Turbopack, written in Rust on top of SWC, replaces Webpack as the default `next dev` bundler in Next.js 15.

Where Webpack re-bundles chunks on every change, Turbopack caches work at the individual function/module level and recomputes only the affected functions. Result: 5-10x faster cold startup on large apps and HMR often under 100ms.


| Feature | Webpack | Turbopack |
|---|---|---|
| Language | JS | Rust |
| Cache granularity | chunk/graph | function/module |
| Cold start (large app) | 6-10s | 1-2s |
| HMR | ~1s | ~50-150ms |
| Config key | `webpack:` | `experimental.turbo` / `turbopack` |
| Prod build | stable | beta in 15.3+ |

**Interview Tip:** Say: Turbopack is not just faster Rust — the win is incremental function-level caching. Interviewers love the before/after numbers and knowing dev is stable, prod is beta.

```js
// next.config.ts — Turbopack config in Next 15
/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */
const nextConfig = {
  // no webpack: needed for most apps
  experimental: {
    turbo: {
      rules: { '*.svg': { loaders: ['@svgr/webpack'], as: '*.js' } }
    }
  }
};
export default nextConfig;
// dev: next dev --turbo  (default in CNA)
// prod beta: next build --turbo
```

