Partial Prerendering lets a single route be hybrid: the static shell (layout, nav, marketing copy) is prerendered at build and served from the CDN, while dynamic islands (personalized greeting, cart, `cookies()`-dependent pricing) are wrapped in `Suspense` and streamed on each request.

In `next.config.js` set `experimental: { ppr: 'incremental' }` (Next 15.3+).


| Strategy | Shell | Dynamic islands | TTFB | Use case |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Static | build | none | fastest | marketing |
| Dynamic | per request | whole page | slow | dashboard |
| PPR | build (CDN) | per-request holes | fast + fresh | e-commerce PLP |
| ISR | build + revalidate | none | fast | blog |

**Interview Tip:** Explain incremental: only Suspense-wrapped dynamic reads become holes. Show `next build` symbols `◐` for PPR — interviewers check adoption knowledge.

```js
// app/page.tsx — PPR incremental example
import { Suspense } from 'react';
import { cookies } from 'next/headers';
async function UserGreeting(){
  const c = await cookies(); // dynamic only inside hole
  const user = c.get('user')?.value ?? 'Guest';
  return <span>Hello {user}</span>;
}
export default function Page(){
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Shop</h1> {/* static shell */}
      <Suspense fallback={<span>Loading greeting…</span>}>
        <UserGreeting />
      </Suspense>
    </main>
  );
}
// next.config.js: experimental:{ ppr:'incremental' }
```

